WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  so  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii this song  that we're going to talk about and  listen to  today was the song of the plush of  inmates it was  sung or rather recited as a poem  by ruth c a native of krakov  poland where she was born in 1920.  during the holocaust ruth survived  several labor camps  and concentration camps in both her  native country  and in germany the inmates  wrote this poem they were quietly  passing it  on to each other as a mean of hope  for survival as a hope to escape  from the deadly cemetery gate  as the song goes  [Music]  here  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  brown  [Music]  [Music]  is  the lyrics of this hymn were related by  a survivor  of the poishuv concentration camp named  ruth cohen  who believes it was written by one of  her fellow inmates  it speaks to the terrible feeling that  prisoners felt at roll call  or oppo plots every morning at 5 am in  the camp  when the prisoners lined up and felt as  if they are one step away from death  the notorious camp commander amon gut  who was portrayed by the actor ray  fines in the film schindler's list would  sometimes  randomly shoot the prisoners at whim  during these roll calls  pashov represented more than the deaths  of individuals  poisson represented the death of the  krakow jewish community  so by thinking about the krakow jewish  community before the holocaust  we can better appreciate the immensity  of the loss  krakow had been a major religious center  throughout the early modern period it  was home  to several lean rabbinical figures such  as  rabbi moses israelis mordechai jaffe  the old circus the visitor to krakow  today  can still see the old synagogues  testament to its former glory the old  jewish cemetery  but by the interwar period krakow jewry  had become much more diverse  krakow was home to theaters cabarets  literary cafes  the great folk song writer mordechai  gueberte  who lived in krakow wrote the song is  brent our town is burning  a defiant song calling on the jews  to rise up and defend themselves during  the pogroms late 1930s  krakow was also home to many hasidic  jews  followers of the great bubba rebbe and  the belzer rebbe  and it was home to a teachers seminary  to train  teachers for orthodox and hasidic girls  and women  krakow was also a major zionist center  and this was the affiliation of ruth  cohen and her family  the great rabbi oziah stone preached  in the temple synagogue and was a  fervent zionist  this same or parliament deputy a  pulinary heart glass  was also a zionist and lived in krakow  in the wake of world war one during the  pogroms that occurred in krakow  the zionists were absolutely crucial to  the formation  of jewish self-defense units  and after this they formed a major  newspaper novogenic which had a  circulation of around 15 000.  zionists held about half the seats on  the kehillah  or the jewish communal self-government  the other half was was held by  hasidic and assimilationist jews there  was also a very popular zionist youth  group named akiva  which would perform a major role  in self-defense during the holocaust  ruth cohen describes her upbringing in  krakow  as occurring between two worlds one  polish  one hebrew ruth attended the zionist  gymnasium where they would learn study  in polish half the day in hebrew the  other half of the day  she thought of herself as really a  zionist  but her family did not have any thoughts  immigrating  to palestine they tried to escape when  the war broke out  but it was just too late  first they lived in the krakow ghetto  and then  on march 13th they were deported along  with most of krakow jews  to the poacher of concentration camp and  by this time there were about 20 000  jews  who were prisoners there and the camp  was run by the notorious  among the sadistic ss commandant  oscar schindler's enamel factory was  located adjacent to the camp  goat and the other guards shot thousands  of jews  and deported thousands more to their  deaths  in auschwitz according to ruth  everyone was afraid all the time but  especially terrified  at 5 00 5 am in the morning when they  would line  up for roll call appel plots  the song the hymn of quasho  reads as follows every morning at 5 the  trumpet wakes us  up with its familiar call to the appa  plots  we stand in straight rows if a hand were  to signal the distance  we would all escape as soon as possible  from the cemetery gate  dark knight hovers over the roofs the  city is sound asleep  only we just one more step and the heart  will die in pain it's especially  poignant to compare this song  to the pre-war song by gabertag is brent  our town is barney  which urged the jews to rise up against  their persecutors  and fight and certainly during the  holocaust  members of the youth group akiva did  just that  however a more representative feeling  is probably captured in this song a  feeling of despair  a feeling of terror a longing for escape  that rarely occurred  [Music]  you

